1
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,878
Hello, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:24,878 --> 00:00:32,006
This is a place for slow stories from the past, told softly, so that you can rest while you listen.

3
00:00:32,006 --> 00:00:38,195
There is nothing you need to remember tonight, and nothing you need to follow closely.

4
00:00:38,195 --> 00:00:43,522
If you drift away halfway through a sentence, that is perfectly all right.

5
00:00:43,522 --> 00:00:50,073
The story will carry on quietly without you, and it will be here again whenever you return.

6
00:00:51,374 --> 00:00:54,222
Before we begin, let yourself settle.

7
00:00:54,222 --> 00:00:58,148
Find the position that feels easiest for your body.

8
00:00:58,148 --> 00:01:03,305
Let your shoulders fall a little lower than they were a moment ago.

9
00:01:03,305 --> 00:01:06,924
Let your hands rest wherever they want to rest.

10
00:01:06,924 --> 00:01:11,618
And take one slow breath in, and let it go, without hurrying.

11
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:17,258
Tonight, we are going to spend an evening in ancient Rome.

12
00:01:17,258 --> 00:01:25,786
Not the Rome of battles and emperors and great speeches, but the quieter city that existed underneath all of that.

13
00:01:25,786 --> 00:01:29,003
The Rome of lamps being lit in small rooms.

14
00:01:29,003 --> 00:01:32,069
Of carts rolling in from the countryside.

15
00:01:32,069 --> 00:01:36,783
Of priestesses tending a fire that was never allowed to go out.

16
00:01:36,783 --> 00:01:41,271
Of ships resting in a harbour, waiting for the morning wind.

17
00:01:42,572 --> 00:01:50,112
We will arrive the way many travellers once arrived, along an old stone road, just as the light is fading.

18
00:01:50,112 --> 00:01:54,453
We will walk into the city as it changes from day into night.

19
00:01:54,453 --> 00:02:04,341
We will pass through the great public square, now mostly empty, and step inside an ordinary home where a family is getting ready for sleep.

20
00:02:04,341 --> 00:02:13,163
Later, we will drift down to the sea, and finally out into the soft hills of the countryside, where the night is very still.

21
00:02:14,462 --> 00:02:22,018
So let the noise of your own day fall behind you now, the way a road falls behind a traveller.

22
00:02:22,018 --> 00:02:26,759
The sky over the hills is turning the colour of old copper.

23
00:02:26,759 --> 00:02:30,134
The first stars are appearing, one by one.

24
00:02:30,134 --> 00:02:36,564
And somewhere ahead of us, in the warm evening air, the city of Rome is waiting.

25
00:02:45,865 --> 00:02:49,326
The road beneath us is called the Appian Way.

26
00:02:49,326 --> 00:02:56,788
It is one of the oldest of all the great Roman roads, and the Romans themselves were proud of it.

27
00:02:56,788 --> 00:03:04,635
A poet once called it the queen of roads, and walking along it in the evening, you can understand why.

28
00:03:04,635 --> 00:03:11,252
It runs straight and patient across the land, as if it has never once been in a hurry.

29
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:21,877
The road was begun more than three hundred years before the time of the emperors, by a Roman official named Appius Claudius.

30
00:03:21,877 --> 00:03:24,435
That is where its name comes from.

31
00:03:24,435 --> 00:03:28,421
At first it ran south from Rome to the city of Capua.

32
00:03:28,421 --> 00:03:41,884
Over the generations it was extended, mile by mile, until it reached all the way to the port of Brundisium, on the heel of Italy, where ships set out across the sea toward Greece.

33
00:03:43,185 --> 00:03:50,479
The surface is made of large, flat stones, fitted closely together like the pieces of a puzzle.

34
00:03:50,479 --> 00:03:56,776
They are dark and smooth, worn down by centuries of wheels and hooves and sandals.

35
00:03:56,776 --> 00:04:01,843
In the fading light, the stones hold a little of the day's warmth.

36
00:04:01,843 --> 00:04:08,677
If you were to rest your hand on one, it would feel faintly warm, like a sleeping animal.

37
00:04:09,978 --> 00:04:15,181
Every so often, at the side of the road, there is a tall stone pillar.

38
00:04:15,181 --> 00:04:16,742
These are milestones.

39
00:04:16,742 --> 00:04:23,508
A Roman mile was a thousand paces, counted as a thousand double steps of a walking soldier.

40
00:04:23,508 --> 00:04:30,346
Each milestone tells the traveller how far they have come, or how far they still have to go.

41
00:04:30,346 --> 00:04:33,172
Tonight, we do not need to count them.

42
00:04:33,172 --> 00:04:39,120
We can simply let them pass, one after another, like slow beats of a quiet song.

43
00:04:40,418 --> 00:04:43,415
On both sides of the road there are tombs.

44
00:04:43,415 --> 00:04:47,978
That might sound strange at first, but it is not a gloomy sight.

45
00:04:47,978 --> 00:05:01,814
Roman law did not allow the dead to be buried inside the city walls, and so families built their monuments along the roads leading out of town, where travellers would pass by and read the names.

46
00:05:01,814 --> 00:05:04,024
Some tombs are small and plain.

47
00:05:04,024 --> 00:05:12,653
Others are grand, round towers of stone, with carved inscriptions asking the passerby to pause for a moment and remember.

48
00:05:13,954 --> 00:05:27,685
Between the tombs grow tall umbrella pines, their flat green tops spread out against the evening sky, and narrow dark cypress trees, pointing upward like quiet candles.

49
00:05:27,685 --> 00:05:37,654
The air smells of warm resin, of dust settling after a long day, and faintly, of wood smoke drifting from somewhere ahead.

50
00:05:38,954 --> 00:05:42,781
There are still a few other travellers on the road.

51
00:05:42,781 --> 00:05:50,209
A farmer walks beside a mule loaded with baskets of vegetables, hoping to sell them in the morning.

52
00:05:50,209 --> 00:05:55,913
A small group of pilgrims talk in low voices, tired from walking since dawn.

53
00:05:55,913 --> 00:06:07,393
A messenger rides past on horseback, a little faster than everyone else, and then the sound of his horse fades away down the road, and it is quiet again.

54
00:06:08,692 --> 00:06:19,185
Along the way there are inns and resting places, where travellers can water their animals and find a simple meal and a bed for the night.

55
00:06:19,185 --> 00:06:20,793
Some are respectable.

56
00:06:20,793 --> 00:06:22,632
Some are a little rough.

57
00:06:22,632 --> 00:06:33,353
But at this hour, most of them glow softly from within, and the murmur of conversation drifts out through their doorways into the warm dusk.

58
00:06:34,653 --> 00:06:38,872
Ahead of us, the walls of the city are coming into view.

59
00:06:38,872 --> 00:06:43,240
The road leads toward an old gate called the Porta Capena.

60
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:47,382
From here, the Appian Way began its long journey south.

61
00:06:47,382 --> 00:06:58,226
And from here, tonight, our journey turns the other way, into the heart of the city, as the last of the daylight slips quietly behind the hills.

62
00:07:07,526 --> 00:07:09,869
Rome at this time was enormous.

63
00:07:09,869 --> 00:07:17,572
Historians often estimate that, at its height, the city may have been home to around a million people.

64
00:07:17,572 --> 00:07:21,348
That is a remarkable number for the ancient world.

65
00:07:21,348 --> 00:07:35,773
Imagine all of those lives, folded together into narrow streets and crowded hillsides, each one with its own small worries, its own small comforts, its own way of getting ready for the night.

66
00:07:37,072 --> 00:07:40,845
Most ordinary Romans did not live in grand houses.

67
00:07:40,845 --> 00:07:54,574
They lived in tall apartment buildings that the Romans called insulae, which means islands, perhaps because each block stood surrounded by streets like an island surrounded by water.

68
00:07:54,574 --> 00:08:01,439
On the ground floor there were often shops and workshops, opening directly onto the street.

69
00:08:01,439 --> 00:08:06,192
Above them, floor after floor, were rooms for families to rent.

70
00:08:07,492 --> 00:08:14,074
In these buildings, the lower floors were usually the most comfortable and the most expensive.

71
00:08:14,074 --> 00:08:18,627
The higher you climbed, the smaller and cheaper the rooms became.

72
00:08:18,627 --> 00:08:24,649
Up near the roof, the rooms were cramped, and hot in summer, and the stairs were long.

73
00:08:24,649 --> 00:08:34,521
But from those top windows, on a clear night, you could see across the rooftops of the whole city, all the way to the dark line of the hills.

74
00:08:35,822 --> 00:08:44,392
The buildings grew so tall that the emperor Augustus set a limit on their height, of about seventy Roman feet.

75
00:08:44,392 --> 00:08:48,989
Later, the emperor Trajan lowered that limit a little more.

76
00:08:48,989 --> 00:09:02,466
Even so, from street level, the insulae must have seemed to rise up on either side like the walls of a canyon, with only a narrow ribbon of evening sky showing between them.

77
00:09:03,768 --> 00:09:07,748
Now, as the light fades, the city changes its rhythm.

78
00:09:07,748 --> 00:09:13,004
The shopkeepers pull wooden shutters across the fronts of their shops.

79
00:09:13,004 --> 00:09:15,932
A baker sweeps flour from his doorstep.

80
00:09:15,932 --> 00:09:19,386
A woman calls her children in from the street.

81
00:09:19,386 --> 00:09:29,900
Someone, high up in one of the buildings, is singing softly while they work, and the sound floats down into the street and then drifts away.

82
00:09:31,201 --> 00:09:35,707
There is something you might not expect about the Roman night.

83
00:09:35,707 --> 00:09:41,378
During the daytime, most wheeled carts were not allowed into the city streets.

84
00:09:41,378 --> 00:09:44,721
The roads were simply too crowded with people.

85
00:09:44,721 --> 00:09:53,299
So the heavy wagons, carrying stone and timber and goods from the countryside, waited outside the gates until evening.

86
00:09:53,299 --> 00:09:56,352
And when the evening came, they rolled in.

87
00:09:57,653 --> 00:10:02,143
So in many neighbourhoods, the night was not completely silent.

88
00:10:02,143 --> 00:10:13,759
There was the slow rumble of wheels over stone, the creak of wooden axles, the patient breathing of oxen, the voices of drivers calling to one another in the dark.

89
00:10:13,759 --> 00:10:19,318
One Roman poet complained that it was impossible to get any sleep in the city.

90
00:10:19,318 --> 00:10:20,816
Perhaps he was right.

91
00:10:20,816 --> 00:10:29,440
But from a distance, from up on a hill, those sounds blend together into a low, steady murmur, like the sound of the sea.

92
00:10:30,740 --> 00:10:35,070
Moving quietly through the streets, there are also the watchmen.

93
00:10:35,070 --> 00:10:44,273
They were called the vigiles, and the emperor Augustus organised them into seven companies, each one responsible for a part of the city.

94
00:10:44,273 --> 00:10:51,716
Their main task was to watch for fires, which were a constant danger in a city of wooden floors and oil lamps.

95
00:10:51,716 --> 00:11:02,610
They carried buckets and ropes and axes, and they walked slowly through the dark streets, looking up at the windows, listening, keeping watch while others slept.

96
00:11:03,912 --> 00:11:07,443
Picture one of them now, pausing at a corner.

97
00:11:07,443 --> 00:11:12,469
He looks up and sees a small light in a window, three floors up.

98
00:11:12,469 --> 00:11:18,749
Someone is still awake, perhaps reading, perhaps mending something by lamplight.

99
00:11:18,749 --> 00:11:20,633
He watches for a moment.

100
00:11:20,633 --> 00:11:22,909
The flame is steady and calm.

101
00:11:22,909 --> 00:11:25,500
He nods to himself, and walks on.

102
00:11:26,801 --> 00:11:30,349
Above it all, the moon has risen over the rooftops.

103
00:11:30,349 --> 00:11:33,201
It turns the tiled roofs pale and silver.

104
00:11:33,201 --> 00:11:45,374
It catches on the rounded shapes of domes, on the tops of columns, on the long arches of the aqueducts that carry fresh water into the city from springs far away in the hills.

105
00:11:45,374 --> 00:11:59,077
The water flows through those channels all night long, quietly, without anyone needing to watch it, filling the public fountains so that in the morning there will be fresh, cool water for everyone.

106
00:12:08,378 --> 00:12:14,540
Before the city sleeps completely, let us pass by one of the great public baths.

107
00:12:14,540 --> 00:12:19,240
For the Romans, the baths were not only a place to get clean.

108
00:12:19,240 --> 00:12:27,019
They were a place to meet friends, to exercise, to talk, to relax, and to simply spend the afternoon.

109
00:12:27,019 --> 00:12:35,109
Even people of very modest means could go, because the entrance fee was tiny, just the smallest of coins.

110
00:12:36,408 --> 00:12:42,046
The baths usually opened in the early afternoon and closed around sunset.

111
00:12:42,046 --> 00:12:46,293
So now, as evening falls, the last bathers are leaving.

112
00:12:46,293 --> 00:12:59,576
They step out through the great doors into the cooler air, their skin warm and soft, their hair still damp, talking quietly as they walk home through the darkening streets.

113
00:13:00,876 --> 00:13:05,421
A Roman bath was a journey through rooms of different temperatures.

114
00:13:05,421 --> 00:13:11,524
First there was a changing room, with niches in the walls where people left their clothes.

115
00:13:11,524 --> 00:13:17,900
Then there was a warm room, gently heated, where you could sit and let your body slowly relax.

116
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:23,124
Then a hot room, full of steam, where the air was thick and heavy and sleepy.

117
00:13:23,124 --> 00:13:30,380
And finally, a cold room with a deep pool, where you could plunge into cool water and come out feeling new.

118
00:13:31,681 --> 00:13:34,375
The rooms were heated in a clever way.

119
00:13:34,375 --> 00:13:40,476
The floors were raised up on small stacks of tiles, leaving a hollow space underneath.

120
00:13:40,476 --> 00:13:54,806
Furnaces at the side of the building pushed hot air through that space, so that the floors themselves grew warm, and in the hottest rooms, so warm that bathers wore wooden sandals to protect their feet.

121
00:13:54,806 --> 00:14:00,693
The warmth rose up through the floor and the walls and filled the rooms from below.

122
00:14:01,994 --> 00:14:04,933
Romans did not use soap the way we do.

123
00:14:04,933 --> 00:14:17,388
Instead, they rubbed olive oil into their skin, and then scraped it away with a curved metal tool called a strigil, taking the dust and sweat of the day with it.

124
00:14:17,388 --> 00:14:28,682
Then they rinsed, and rested, and perhaps had a massage, or simply lay quietly in the warmth, listening to the echo of water in the vaulted halls.

125
00:14:29,981 --> 00:14:32,725
Now, though, the halls are emptying.

126
00:14:32,725 --> 00:14:40,729
The attendants walk slowly through the rooms, gathering forgotten towels, wiping down the marble benches.

127
00:14:40,729 --> 00:14:42,786
The great pools grow still.

128
00:14:42,786 --> 00:14:49,798
Their surfaces become perfectly smooth, reflecting the high ceilings and the last few lamps.

129
00:14:49,798 --> 00:14:57,267
The echoes fade, one by one, until the only sound is the slow drip of water somewhere in the dark.

130
00:14:58,567 --> 00:15:05,018
Down below, beside the furnaces, the stokers let the fires burn lower for the night.

131
00:15:05,018 --> 00:15:13,386
The heat will linger in the stones for hours, slowly fading, like the warmth of a bed after you have left it.

132
00:15:13,386 --> 00:15:20,682
Tomorrow afternoon, the fires will be fed again, and the halls will fill with voices and steam.

133
00:15:20,682 --> 00:15:23,677
But tonight, the baths are resting too.

134
00:15:32,976 --> 00:15:37,750
Let us walk now toward the centre of the city, to the Forum.

135
00:15:37,750 --> 00:15:41,808
During the day, this was the busiest place in Rome.

136
00:15:41,808 --> 00:15:56,849
It was a great open space, surrounded by temples and law courts and meeting halls, filled with people arguing, buying, selling, listening to speeches, and simply watching one another go by.

137
00:15:58,149 --> 00:16:02,162
But at night, the Forum is a very different place.

138
00:16:02,162 --> 00:16:04,249
The crowds have gone home.

139
00:16:04,249 --> 00:16:06,014
The voices have faded.

140
00:16:06,014 --> 00:16:14,602
The tall columns stand in long, quiet rows, casting soft shadows across the paving stones in the moonlight.

141
00:16:14,602 --> 00:16:23,833
Here and there a cat moves silently across the open ground, and disappears into the darkness between two buildings.

142
00:16:25,134 --> 00:16:30,390
At one end of the Forum stands the speakers' platform, called the Rostra.

143
00:16:30,390 --> 00:16:35,576
During the day, famous orators stood here and spoke to the crowds below.

144
00:16:35,576 --> 00:16:36,729
Now it is empty.

145
00:16:36,729 --> 00:16:44,289
If you stood on it tonight, there would be no one to hear you, and perhaps that would feel like a relief.

146
00:16:44,289 --> 00:16:46,955
Some places are made for many voices.

147
00:16:46,955 --> 00:16:51,202
But they are also beautiful when they are completely still.

148
00:16:52,503 --> 00:16:56,346
Not far away stands the Senate house, the Curia.

149
00:16:56,346 --> 00:17:00,750
It is a tall, simple building, with heavy bronze doors.

150
00:17:00,750 --> 00:17:08,436
Inside, during the day, the senators sat on long benches and debated the business of the empire.

151
00:17:08,436 --> 00:17:19,404
Tonight the doors are closed, and the great room inside is dark and cool and silent, holding all of those old words quietly in its walls.

152
00:17:20,704 --> 00:17:28,632
Near the temple of Saturn, there is a small monument that the Romans called the Golden Milestone.

153
00:17:28,632 --> 00:17:41,462
It was set up by the emperor Augustus, and it was said to mark the symbolic centre of the Roman road network, the place from which all the great roads began.

154
00:17:41,462 --> 00:17:45,631
There is an old saying that all roads lead to Rome.

155
00:17:45,631 --> 00:18:03,365
Standing here, in the quiet of the night, it is pleasant to imagine all of those roads, stretching away in every direction across the dark countryside, and all of them, eventually, leading back to this one small spot.

156
00:18:04,664 --> 00:18:12,075
And now, at the edge of the Forum, there is a small round temple, glowing softly from within.

157
00:18:12,075 --> 00:18:17,491
This is the temple of Vesta, the goddess of the hearth and the home.

158
00:18:17,491 --> 00:18:22,671
It is one of the oldest and most sacred places in the whole city.

159
00:18:23,971 --> 00:18:29,606
Inside this temple burns a fire, and that fire is never allowed to go out.

160
00:18:29,606 --> 00:18:37,680
The Romans believed that as long as the sacred flame of Vesta kept burning, the city itself would be safe.

161
00:18:37,680 --> 00:18:44,153
The fire was looked after by a small group of priestesses, called the Vestal Virgins.

162
00:18:44,153 --> 00:18:50,551
There were only six of them at a time, and they served the goddess for thirty years.

163
00:18:51,852 --> 00:18:55,042
Imagine one of them now, at this late hour.

164
00:18:55,042 --> 00:19:02,981
She moves quietly around the hearth, adding a little wood to the fire, watching the flames settle and glow.

165
00:19:02,981 --> 00:19:06,394
The light flickers gently on the curved walls.

166
00:19:06,394 --> 00:19:19,009
Outside, the whole city is falling asleep, but here, in this small round room, someone is awake, keeping the flame alive, as it has been kept alive for hundreds of years.

167
00:19:20,308 --> 00:19:24,020
There is something very calming about that idea.

168
00:19:24,020 --> 00:19:29,278
Somewhere, someone is watching over the fire, so you do not have to.

169
00:19:29,278 --> 00:19:33,531
You can let go of whatever you were holding onto today.

170
00:19:33,531 --> 00:19:36,470
Someone else is keeping watch tonight.

171
00:19:36,470 --> 00:19:37,862
The flame is safe.

172
00:19:37,862 --> 00:19:39,175
The city is safe.

173
00:19:39,175 --> 00:19:40,490
And you can rest.

174
00:19:41,790 --> 00:19:46,034
The moon moves slowly across the sky above the Forum.

175
00:19:46,034 --> 00:19:51,798
The shadows of the columns shift, a little at a time, across the stones.

176
00:19:51,798 --> 00:20:01,886
A breeze moves through the open square, carrying the faint scent of the fire from the temple, and then that, too, grows still.

177
00:20:11,186 --> 00:20:15,856
Let us leave the open spaces now, and step inside a home.

178
00:20:15,856 --> 00:20:22,085
Not a palace, but a comfortable house belonging to a family of modest means.

179
00:20:22,085 --> 00:20:31,260
The street door is heavy and wooden, and once it closes behind us, the sounds of the city grow soft and distant.

180
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:37,156
Roman houses of this kind often looked inward rather than outward.

181
00:20:37,156 --> 00:20:41,613
From the street, they showed only plain walls and a single door.

182
00:20:41,613 --> 00:20:50,317
But inside, the rooms were arranged around an open hall called the atrium, with a gap in the roof that let in light and rain.

183
00:20:50,317 --> 00:20:56,234
Beneath that opening, in the floor, there was a shallow basin to catch the rainwater.

184
00:20:56,234 --> 00:21:05,287
Tonight, the moon is shining through the opening in the roof, and its reflection lies perfectly still on the surface of the water.

185
00:21:06,586 --> 00:21:13,000
The main meal of the day, the cena, was eaten in the late afternoon or early evening.

186
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,791
The family has already eaten tonight.

187
00:21:15,791 --> 00:21:23,789
There was bread, and olives, and lentils cooked with herbs, and a little cheese, and some fruit to finish.

188
00:21:23,789 --> 00:21:26,355
The dishes have been cleared away.

189
00:21:26,355 --> 00:21:32,164
In the kitchen, the fire in the hearth has burned down to a soft orange glow.

190
00:21:33,464 --> 00:21:36,290
Now the house is lit by small oil lamps.

191
00:21:36,290 --> 00:21:46,392
These were made of clay, or sometimes of bronze, shaped a little like a flattened teapot, with a spout at one end where a wick of linen rested.

192
00:21:46,392 --> 00:21:51,690
They were filled with olive oil, and they gave a small, warm, steady flame.

193
00:21:51,690 --> 00:21:55,079
It was not a bright light, not by our standards.

194
00:21:55,079 --> 00:22:00,942
But it was enough to read by, enough to see a face, enough to find your way to bed.

195
00:22:02,242 --> 00:22:08,663
In a small room off the atrium, the father of the family is sitting at a table with a scroll.

196
00:22:08,663 --> 00:22:17,291
Books in Rome were not bound pages but long rolls, which you unrolled with one hand and rolled up with the other as you read.

197
00:22:17,291 --> 00:22:23,711
Romans often read aloud, even when they were alone, softly murmuring the words to themselves.

198
00:22:23,711 --> 00:22:32,962
So if you listen carefully, you can hear him now, reading a few lines of poetry in a low voice, slowly, as if he is tasting each word.

199
00:22:34,262 --> 00:22:38,061
The Romans counted time differently from the way we do.

200
00:22:38,061 --> 00:22:43,242
They divided the daylight into twelve hours, and the darkness into watches.

201
00:22:43,242 --> 00:22:50,220
This meant that a summer hour was longer than a winter hour, because the days themselves were longer.

202
00:22:50,220 --> 00:22:54,778
Time stretched and shrank with the seasons, like something living.

203
00:22:54,778 --> 00:22:58,163
At night, there was no clock ticking on the wall.

204
00:22:58,163 --> 00:23:04,449
There was only the slow movement of the stars, and the oil slowly burning down in the lamp.

205
00:23:05,749 --> 00:23:10,243
Elsewhere in the house, the children have already gone to bed.

206
00:23:10,243 --> 00:23:14,230
Roman bedrooms, called cubicula, were small and simple.

207
00:23:14,230 --> 00:23:24,307
Often there was just a narrow bed, a wooden frame with a mattress stuffed with wool or straw, a blanket, perhaps a small chest for clothes.

208
00:23:24,307 --> 00:23:27,931
These rooms were not for living in during the day.

209
00:23:27,931 --> 00:23:33,440
They were only for sleeping, and so they were kept dark and quiet and close.

210
00:23:34,740 --> 00:23:41,674
Most Romans went to bed early, not long after dark, and rose with the first light of morning.

211
00:23:41,674 --> 00:23:44,731
There was a kind of natural rhythm to it.

212
00:23:44,731 --> 00:23:48,981
When the sun went down, the work of the day was finished.

213
00:23:48,981 --> 00:23:53,455
When the lamps were blown out, the house settled into sleep.

214
00:23:53,455 --> 00:23:58,228
And when the sky began to grey in the east, the day began again.

215
00:23:59,528 --> 00:24:04,378
In the kitchen, an old servant makes one last round of the house.

216
00:24:04,378 --> 00:24:08,558
She checks that the fire in the hearth is safely banked.

217
00:24:08,558 --> 00:24:11,916
She closes a shutter that has been left open.

218
00:24:11,916 --> 00:24:19,753
She pauses in the atrium and looks up at the square of moonlit sky in the roof, and listens to the quiet.

219
00:24:19,753 --> 00:24:24,231
Somewhere in the house, someone sighs softly in their sleep.

220
00:24:25,531 --> 00:24:30,425
Back in the study, the father has come to the end of his reading.

221
00:24:30,425 --> 00:24:35,619
He rolls the scroll up slowly and carefully, and ties it with a cord.

222
00:24:35,619 --> 00:24:41,792
He sits for a moment in the soft light, not thinking about anything in particular.

223
00:24:41,792 --> 00:24:45,782
Then he leans forward, and gently blows out the lamp.

224
00:24:45,782 --> 00:24:49,848
A thin line of smoke rises in the dark and curls away.

225
00:24:49,848 --> 00:24:51,579
And the house is still.

226
00:25:00,880 --> 00:25:06,746
Let us step outside again, into the cool night, and walk down toward the river.

227
00:25:06,746 --> 00:25:14,318
The Tiber flows through the heart of Rome, wide and slow, curving gently around the base of the hills.

228
00:25:14,318 --> 00:25:23,451
It is the reason the city grew here in the first place, where the river could be crossed and where boats could come and go.

229
00:25:24,750 --> 00:25:31,347
In the middle of the river there is a small island, long and narrow, with pointed ends.

230
00:25:31,347 --> 00:25:47,044
The Romans said it looked like a ship, and at some point they even faced its stone edges to look like the bow of a boat, as if the whole island were sailing slowly down the river and never arriving anywhere.

231
00:25:48,344 --> 00:25:52,730
On that island stood a temple to Aesculapius, the god of healing.

232
00:25:52,730 --> 00:26:00,962
The story goes that, long ago, during a time of sickness, the Romans sent a ship to Greece to bring the god to their city.

233
00:26:00,962 --> 00:26:06,362
On the way home, a sacred serpent slipped from the ship and swam to this island.

234
00:26:06,362 --> 00:26:10,208
The Romans took it as a sign, and built the temple there.

235
00:26:10,208 --> 00:26:21,005
For centuries afterward, people who were unwell came to the island to rest and to recover, sleeping in the quiet halls of the temple, hoping for healing dreams.

236
00:26:22,305 --> 00:26:25,919
There is something gentle in that idea, too.

237
00:26:25,919 --> 00:26:30,435
An island in the middle of a river, set aside for rest.

238
00:26:30,435 --> 00:26:37,579
A place where people came simply to lie down, and sleep, and let time do its slow work.

239
00:26:38,880 --> 00:26:45,491
Crossing from the city to the island is a stone bridge with two wide, graceful arches.

240
00:26:45,491 --> 00:26:56,102
It was built in the last years before the emperors, and it is so well made that it still stands today, more than two thousand years later.

241
00:26:56,102 --> 00:26:58,255
People still walk across it.

242
00:26:58,255 --> 00:27:06,634
If you were to visit Rome now, you could stand on the very same stones, and look down at the very same river.

243
00:27:07,934 --> 00:27:10,311
Tonight, the bridge is empty.

244
00:27:10,311 --> 00:27:25,065
The moonlight lies along its curved arches and their reflections in the water below, so that each arch forms a perfect circle, half made of stone, and half made of water and light.

245
00:27:26,365 --> 00:27:35,041
Along the riverbank, small boats are tied up for the night, bumping softly against one another as the current moves past.

246
00:27:35,041 --> 00:27:43,718
A fisherman sits on the bank with a line in the water, not really expecting to catch anything, simply enjoying the quiet.

247
00:27:43,718 --> 00:27:49,383
After a while, he winds in his line, gathers his things, and walks slowly home.

248
00:27:49,383 --> 00:27:56,338
And the river flows on alone, past the island, past the bridges, out of the city, toward the sea.

249
00:28:05,638 --> 00:28:15,347
Let us follow the river now, as it winds its way westward, out of the city and across the flat green land toward the coast.

250
00:28:15,347 --> 00:28:19,135
Beyond the walls, the banks grow wide and empty.

251
00:28:19,135 --> 00:28:29,318
Reeds rustle softly at the water's edge, and moonlight lies on the river in long, broken ribbons, moving gently with the current.

252
00:28:30,618 --> 00:28:36,287
At the mouth of the river lay the town of Ostia, the harbour of Rome.

253
00:28:36,287 --> 00:28:43,597
A city of a million people needed an enormous amount of food, and much of it came by sea.

254
00:28:43,597 --> 00:28:53,701
Ships arrived here from all across the Mediterranean, carrying wine and olive oil, timber and marble, and above all, grain.

255
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:05,518
Much of that grain came from Egypt, where the river Nile flooded every year and left behind rich, dark soil that grew wheat in great abundance.

256
00:29:05,518 --> 00:29:10,151
Large merchant ships carried the grain across the sea to Italy.

257
00:29:10,151 --> 00:29:18,240
These were wide, round-bellied ships with a great square sail, and they were slow and steady rather than fast.

258
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:23,608
When they appeared on the horizon, people knew that bread was on its way.

259
00:29:24,909 --> 00:29:31,334
Over time the harbour at Ostia became too small and too shallow for so many ships.

260
00:29:31,334 --> 00:29:41,440
So the emperor Claudius began building a great new harbour a little to the north, with long stone arms reaching out into the sea.

261
00:29:41,440 --> 00:29:51,939
Later, the emperor Trajan added an inner basin, shaped like a hexagon, where ships could rest in calm water, protected from the waves.

262
00:29:53,239 --> 00:29:57,425
At the entrance to Claudius' harbour there stood a lighthouse.

263
00:29:57,425 --> 00:30:01,614
And there is a lovely story about how its foundation was made.

264
00:30:01,614 --> 00:30:08,840
A huge ship had once been built to carry an obelisk, a tall stone monument, all the way from Egypt to Rome.

265
00:30:08,840 --> 00:30:17,012
When its work was done, that ship was filled with stone and sunk into the sea, and the lighthouse was built on top of it.

266
00:30:17,012 --> 00:30:24,982
So the light that guided sailors home at night stood on the back of an old ship, resting at the bottom of the harbour.

267
00:30:26,282 --> 00:30:31,121
Tonight, the lighthouse glows at the edge of the dark water.

268
00:30:31,121 --> 00:30:36,362
Its fire is small at this distance, a single warm point of light.

269
00:30:36,362 --> 00:30:45,069
But to a sailor out at sea, tired from many days of travel, it would be the most welcome sight in the world.

270
00:30:45,069 --> 00:30:48,296
It means the long voyage is nearly over.

271
00:30:48,296 --> 00:30:50,068
It means rest is near.

272
00:30:51,368 --> 00:30:55,463
In the harbour, the ships are anchored for the night.

273
00:30:55,463 --> 00:30:57,240
Their sails are furled.

274
00:30:57,240 --> 00:31:09,835
The water laps softly against their wooden sides, and their masts sway very slowly, back and forth, back and forth, as the gentle swell rolls in from the open sea.

275
00:31:09,835 --> 00:31:16,479
On board, most of the sailors are already asleep, rocked by the movement of the water.

276
00:31:17,778 --> 00:31:25,189
Along the quays stand great warehouses, called horrea, where the grain and other goods were stored.

277
00:31:25,189 --> 00:31:31,401
They are long, solid buildings, with thick walls to keep out the damp and the heat.

278
00:31:31,401 --> 00:31:43,976
Inside, in the dark, the sacks and jars stand in rows, waiting patiently for morning, when they will be loaded onto river barges and pulled slowly up the Tiber to Rome.

279
00:31:45,276 --> 00:31:54,368
Those barges were often towed upstream by teams of oxen walking along the riverbank, or by men pulling on long ropes.

280
00:31:54,368 --> 00:31:56,311
It was slow, steady work.

281
00:31:56,311 --> 00:32:09,211
But tonight, the oxen are resting in their stalls, and the ropes are coiled on the quays, and the river flows on without them, carrying the moonlight down to the sea.

282
00:32:10,511 --> 00:32:14,193
Stand here for a moment at the edge of the water.

283
00:32:14,193 --> 00:32:17,500
Feel the cool breeze coming in from the sea.

284
00:32:17,500 --> 00:32:22,084
Listen to the waves, rolling in, and slipping back out again.

285
00:32:22,084 --> 00:32:22,986
In, and out.

286
00:32:22,986 --> 00:32:23,886
In, and out.

287
00:32:23,886 --> 00:32:25,390
Like slow breathing.

288
00:32:25,390 --> 00:32:35,835
The sea has been breathing like this for longer than anyone can remember, and it will go on breathing like this long after tonight is over.

289
00:32:45,134 --> 00:32:55,345
Now let us leave the coast behind, and drift inland, over fields and olive groves, into the soft hills of the countryside.

290
00:32:55,345 --> 00:33:01,454
Here, away from the city and the harbour, the night is very quiet indeed.

291
00:33:02,755 --> 00:33:07,788
Many wealthy Romans owned houses in the country, called villas.

292
00:33:07,788 --> 00:33:13,621
Some were working farms, with vineyards and orchards and fields of grain.

293
00:33:13,621 --> 00:33:24,087
Others were places of retreat, where people went to escape the noise and heat of the city, to read, to walk, to think, and to rest.

294
00:33:25,387 --> 00:33:35,319
A Roman writer named Pliny the Younger loved his country house near the sea, and he wrote letters describing it in loving detail.

295
00:33:35,319 --> 00:33:40,864
He wrote about its sunny rooms, its gardens, and the views of the water.

296
00:33:40,864 --> 00:33:50,180
And he described one small room, set a little apart from the rest of the house, where he could go to be completely alone.

297
00:33:50,180 --> 00:34:03,653
During the noisy midwinter festival, when the rest of the household was celebrating, he would retreat to that room, where he could hear none of the noise, and enjoy the quiet.

298
00:34:04,953 --> 00:34:08,646
There is a villa on the hillside now, below us.

299
00:34:08,646 --> 00:34:12,338
A few of its windows still glow with lamplight.

300
00:34:12,338 --> 00:34:16,502
Inside, the household is settling down for the night.

301
00:34:16,502 --> 00:34:19,252
The farm animals are in their pens.

302
00:34:19,252 --> 00:34:22,002
The dogs are curled up by the door.

303
00:34:22,002 --> 00:34:29,465
Out in the fields, the vines stand in long, patient rows, their leaves silver in the moonlight.

304
00:34:30,766 --> 00:34:34,875
The tall cypress trees stand still against the sky.

305
00:34:34,875 --> 00:34:49,134
A soft wind moves through the olive trees, and their leaves turn over, showing their pale undersides, so that the whole grove seems to shimmer gently, and then grows dark again.

306
00:34:50,434 --> 00:34:53,884
Somewhere in the valley, a nightingale is singing.

307
00:34:53,884 --> 00:34:56,161
The Romans loved the nightingale.

308
00:34:56,161 --> 00:35:11,067
Pliny the Elder, the uncle of Pliny the Younger, wrote in his great book about the natural world that in spring, when the leaves are thickening, the nightingale sings without stopping for many days and nights on end.

309
00:35:11,067 --> 00:35:16,449
Its song is long and varied, rising and falling, pausing, and beginning again.

310
00:35:16,449 --> 00:35:19,416
It is not singing for anyone in particular.

311
00:35:19,416 --> 00:35:21,900
It is simply singing into the night.

312
00:35:23,201 --> 00:35:29,012
Above the hills, the stars are very bright now, far brighter than in the city.

313
00:35:29,012 --> 00:35:32,065
The Romans looked up at these same stars.

314
00:35:32,065 --> 00:35:35,791
They gave them names, and told stories about them.

315
00:35:35,791 --> 00:35:47,114
They watched them turn slowly across the sky through the long night watches, and used them to tell the seasons, and to guide their ships across the sea.

316
00:35:48,414 --> 00:35:52,840
Those same stars are above you tonight, wherever you are.

317
00:35:52,840 --> 00:36:06,425
They are turning slowly over your roof, just as they turned over the rooftops of Rome, over the temple of Vesta, over the harbour at Ostia, over this quiet villa in the hills.

318
00:36:06,425 --> 00:36:12,478
Two thousand years have passed, and yet the night sky is very nearly the same.

319
00:36:13,778 --> 00:36:17,010
In the villa, the last lamp is blown out.

320
00:36:17,010 --> 00:36:18,588
The windows go dark.

321
00:36:18,588 --> 00:36:21,032
The house settles into silence.

322
00:36:21,032 --> 00:36:26,550
The fields and the vineyards and the olive groves rest under the moon.

323
00:36:26,550 --> 00:36:36,009
And the long, quiet road we travelled earlier stretches away across the countryside, empty now, waiting for the morning.

324
00:36:37,309 --> 00:36:40,786
There is nowhere else you need to be tonight.

325
00:36:40,786 --> 00:36:42,951
Nothing else you need to do.

326
00:36:42,951 --> 00:36:51,452
You can let the hills hold you, and the stars watch over you, and the old stories carry on softly without you.

327
00:36:52,753 --> 00:36:57,577
Thank you for spending this evening with The Quiet Archive.

328
00:36:57,577 --> 00:36:58,394
Rest well.

329
00:36:58,394 --> 00:36:59,458
Sleep deeply.

330
00:36:59,458 --> 00:37:00,603
And goodnight.

331
00:37:56,833 --> 00:38:00,944
Good evening, and welcome back to The Quiet Archive.

332
00:38:00,944 --> 00:38:05,214
Tonight's story is a slow one, and that is on purpose.

333
00:38:05,214 --> 00:38:08,139
You do not need to follow every word.

334
00:38:08,139 --> 00:38:11,381
If your thoughts wander, let them wander.

335
00:38:11,381 --> 00:38:16,758
If you fall asleep before the end, that is the best possible ending.

336
00:38:18,058 --> 00:38:21,357
Settle into the place where you are resting.

337
00:38:21,357 --> 00:38:24,657
Let the pillow take the weight of your head.

338
00:38:24,657 --> 00:38:32,906
Let your jaw loosen, and your forehead soften, and let one long breath leave your body, as slowly as it likes.

339
00:38:34,206 --> 00:38:45,462
Tonight we are going to spend an evening and a night inside a monastery, somewhere in the countryside of Europe, around eight or nine hundred years ago.

340
00:38:45,462 --> 00:38:49,980
A place built for prayer, for work, and above all, for quiet.

341
00:38:49,980 --> 00:38:57,459
A place where the day was measured not by clocks, but by bells, and by the slow turning of the light.

342
00:38:58,759 --> 00:39:01,848
We will arrive at the gate just before dusk.

343
00:39:01,848 --> 00:39:08,866
We will hear the evening prayers, share a simple supper, and walk the covered paths of the cloister.

344
00:39:08,866 --> 00:39:16,166
We will visit the room where books were copied by hand, and the garden where herbs and bees were tended.

345
00:39:16,166 --> 00:39:23,677
And when the last prayers of the day are sung, we will follow the monks into the deep silence of the night.

346
00:39:32,977 --> 00:39:38,941
The road to the monastery climbs gently through fields and low stone walls.

347
00:39:38,941 --> 00:39:47,290
The sun has already slipped behind the hills, and the sky is turning from pale gold to a soft, cool blue.

348
00:39:47,290 --> 00:39:55,163
Somewhere ahead, a single bell is ringing, slow and unhurried, calling everyone in from their work.

349
00:39:56,463 --> 00:40:01,675
The monastery itself sits in a sheltered valley, close to a stream.

350
00:40:01,675 --> 00:40:09,376
Monasteries were often built in places like this, near running water, away from the noise of towns.

351
00:40:09,376 --> 00:40:12,176
From the road you can see its shape.

352
00:40:12,176 --> 00:40:23,066
A long stone church with a square tower, a cluster of low buildings around it, an orchard, and the dark line of a wall that encloses it all.

353
00:40:24,366 --> 00:40:28,885
At the gate there is a small lodge, where the porter lives.

354
00:40:28,885 --> 00:40:34,400
He is an older monk, chosen for this job because he is patient and kind.

355
00:40:34,400 --> 00:40:46,808
The rule the monks follow says that the porter should be someone wise enough to receive a message and give a sensible answer, and steady enough not to wander off.

356
00:40:48,109 --> 00:40:53,549
That rule was written centuries earlier by a man named Benedict, in Italy.

357
00:40:53,549 --> 00:41:00,827
It is a short book, practical and gentle, describing how a community of monks should live together.

358
00:41:00,827 --> 00:41:08,546
How they should pray, how they should eat, how much they should sleep, how they should treat one another.

359
00:41:08,546 --> 00:41:13,325
Monasteries all over Europe followed it, and many still do today.

360
00:41:14,625 --> 00:41:23,924
One of the things the rule asks is that guests should be welcomed warmly, as if they were Christ himself arriving at the door.

361
00:41:23,924 --> 00:41:33,076
So when you knock, the porter opens the little window in the gate, looks out at you with a tired smile, and says a blessing.

362
00:41:33,076 --> 00:41:41,121
Then the heavy door swings open, and you step inside, out of the wide evening, into a smaller, quieter world.

363
00:41:42,420 --> 00:41:45,972
Inside the wall, everything seems to slow down.

364
00:41:45,972 --> 00:41:47,333
The path is swept.

365
00:41:47,333 --> 00:41:50,582
A cat sits on a low wall, washing its paws.

366
00:41:50,582 --> 00:42:01,388
Somewhere a cart is being unloaded, and a lay brother, one of the working members of the community, is leading a tired horse toward the stable.

367
00:42:01,388 --> 00:42:03,127
The bell stops ringing.

368
00:42:03,127 --> 00:42:10,986
And in the stillness that follows, you can hear the stream running softly on the other side of the wall.

369
00:42:20,286 --> 00:42:25,672
The bell you heard was calling the monks to Vespers, the evening prayer.

370
00:42:25,672 --> 00:42:35,622
In the monastery, the day was divided into a series of short services, spread from the middle of the night to the end of the evening.

371
00:42:35,622 --> 00:42:42,131
Vespers came as the light was fading, and it was one of the most beautiful of them all.

372
00:42:43,431 --> 00:42:46,288
The church is cool inside, and dim.

373
00:42:46,288 --> 00:42:54,777
The last daylight comes through narrow windows high in the walls, and a few candles burn near the altar.

374
00:42:54,777 --> 00:43:04,573
The monks file in quietly and take their places in wooden stalls, facing one another across the choir, in two long rows.

375
00:43:05,872 --> 00:43:07,629
Then they begin to sing.

376
00:43:07,629 --> 00:43:13,339
It is a single melody, without harmony, rising and falling like a slow breath.

377
00:43:13,339 --> 00:43:16,413
This kind of singing is called plainchant.

378
00:43:16,413 --> 00:43:27,320
The words are psalms, ancient songs of praise and longing, sung one verse on this side of the choir, and the next verse answered from the other side.

379
00:43:27,320 --> 00:43:28,418
Back and forth.

380
00:43:28,418 --> 00:43:29,516
Back and forth.

381
00:43:29,516 --> 00:43:33,542
Like waves arriving on a shore and slipping away again.

382
00:43:34,842 --> 00:43:39,565
You do not need to understand the words to feel the shape of it.

383
00:43:39,565 --> 00:43:48,053
The voices fill the stone room and linger in the air after each phrase, as if the building itself is singing along.

384
00:43:48,053 --> 00:43:56,246
Over the years, many of these monks will have sung every one of the hundred and fifty psalms hundreds of times.

385
00:43:56,246 --> 00:43:59,567
They know them the way you know the way home.

386
00:44:00,867 --> 00:44:05,540
After Vespers, the community walks to the refectory for supper.

387
00:44:05,540 --> 00:44:09,100
This is a long hall with tables along the walls.

388
00:44:09,100 --> 00:44:10,509
The meal is simple.

389
00:44:10,509 --> 00:44:19,187
Bread, a dish of beans or lentils, some vegetables from the garden, perhaps cheese or fish, and a cup of wine or ale.

390
00:44:19,187 --> 00:44:31,203
The rule of Benedict allowed a modest amount to drink, and asked for two cooked dishes at the main meal, so that anyone who could not eat one might eat the other.

391
00:44:32,503 --> 00:44:34,751
Nobody talks during the meal.

392
00:44:34,751 --> 00:44:45,838
Instead, one monk stands at a wooden reading desk and reads aloud, from scripture or from the lives of saints, while the others eat in silence.

393
00:44:45,838 --> 00:44:47,931
His voice is calm and even.

394
00:44:47,931 --> 00:44:49,404
Spoons touch bowls.

395
00:44:49,404 --> 00:44:51,652
Bread is passed hand to hand.

396
00:44:51,652 --> 00:45:00,258
The candles burn low on the tables, and outside the windows, the sky turns from blue to the deep colour of ink.

397
00:45:01,558 --> 00:45:09,662
If a monk needs something during the meal, the salt or the water jug, he does not ask for it out loud.

398
00:45:09,662 --> 00:45:17,687
Monasteries developed simple hand signs for everyday things, so that the silence would not be broken.

399
00:45:17,687 --> 00:45:19,276
A gesture for bread.

400
00:45:19,276 --> 00:45:20,785
A gesture for fish.

401
00:45:20,785 --> 00:45:23,963
A gesture for please, and for thank you.

402
00:45:23,963 --> 00:45:28,651
Imagine a whole room speaking softly with only their hands.

403
00:45:37,951 --> 00:45:43,809
After supper, there is a little time before the last prayers of the day.

404
00:45:43,809 --> 00:45:46,576
Let us step out into the cloister.

405
00:45:46,576 --> 00:45:49,424
This is the heart of the monastery.

406
00:45:49,424 --> 00:45:58,943
A square garden, open to the sky, surrounded on all four sides by a covered walkway, held up by rows of stone arches.

407
00:46:00,243 --> 00:46:03,354
The church forms one side of the square.

408
00:46:03,354 --> 00:46:17,354
On the other sides are the chapter house, where the monks meet each morning, the refectory where we just ate, and above one range of buildings, the long dormitory where they sleep.

409
00:46:17,354 --> 00:46:23,731
Everything important in their daily life is gathered around this one quiet garden.

410
00:46:25,031 --> 00:46:29,978
The moon has risen now, and it shines down into the open square.

411
00:46:29,978 --> 00:46:37,243
Between the arches, the light falls in long pale shapes across the stone floor of the walkway.

412
00:46:37,243 --> 00:46:38,248
Arch, shadow.

413
00:46:38,248 --> 00:46:39,253
Arch, shadow.

414
00:46:39,253 --> 00:46:49,842
Walking slowly along the path, you pass from light into darkness and back into light, again and again, at the pace of your own breathing.

415
00:46:51,142 --> 00:46:59,162
In the middle of the garden, there is often a well, or a small fountain, or simply a square of grass.

416
00:46:59,162 --> 00:47:03,052
Some cloisters had a single tree at their centre.

417
00:47:03,052 --> 00:47:11,707
The idea was to create a picture of paradise, a garden enclosed and protected, where the mind could be still.

418
00:47:13,007 --> 00:47:17,934
During the day, the cloister walkways were busy with quiet work.

419
00:47:17,934 --> 00:47:23,784
Monks sat on stone benches along the walls to read, or to study, or to copy.

420
00:47:23,784 --> 00:47:30,019
Some cloisters had little wooden desks built into the arches, close to the light.

421
00:47:30,019 --> 00:47:33,944
Older monks taught younger ones their letters here.

422
00:47:33,944 --> 00:47:39,333
Others simply walked, slowly, thinking, or praying under their breath.

423
00:47:40,632 --> 00:47:44,123
Now, at the end of the day, it is almost empty.

424
00:47:44,123 --> 00:47:52,739
One monk is sitting on a bench in the corner with his hands folded in his sleeves, looking at nothing in particular.

425
00:47:52,739 --> 00:47:57,195
Another walks the length of one side, turns, and walks back.

426
00:47:57,195 --> 00:48:00,537
Their sandals make a soft sound on the stone.

427
00:48:00,537 --> 00:48:08,707
A breeze moves across the garden, and the moonlight trembles a little on the surface of the water in the well.

428
00:48:18,007 --> 00:48:24,758
Just off the cloister there is a room we should visit before it closes for the night.

429
00:48:24,758 --> 00:48:29,285
This is the scriptorium, the place where books were made.

430
00:48:29,285 --> 00:48:38,657
In a world without printing, every single book had to be copied by hand, letter by letter, line by line, page by page.

431
00:48:38,657 --> 00:48:42,946
A single large book might take one person many months.

432
00:48:44,246 --> 00:48:52,281
The pages were not made of paper, but of parchment, prepared from the skins of calves, sheep, or goats.

433
00:48:52,281 --> 00:49:03,046
The skin was soaked, scraped, stretched on a frame, and dried, until it became a thin, smooth, pale surface that would last for centuries.

434
00:49:03,046 --> 00:49:07,649
The very finest kind, made from calfskin, is called vellum.

435
00:49:08,948 --> 00:49:15,370
The ink was often made from oak galls, small round growths that form on oak trees.

436
00:49:15,370 --> 00:49:21,008
They were crushed and soaked, and mixed with a little iron salt and gum.

437
00:49:21,008 --> 00:49:28,447
The ink went onto the page a pale brown, and slowly darkened to a deep, rich black as it dried.

438
00:49:28,447 --> 00:49:35,103
Many of these pages are still perfectly legible today, nearly a thousand years later.

439
00:49:36,403 --> 00:49:41,603
The scribes wrote with quills cut from the feathers of geese or swans.

440
00:49:41,603 --> 00:49:52,969
A good scribe kept a small knife beside him, to sharpen the quill as it wore down, and to gently scrape away a mistake from the surface of the parchment.

441
00:49:52,969 --> 00:50:00,621
In the margins of some old manuscripts you can still find little notes the scribes wrote to themselves.

442
00:50:00,621 --> 00:50:01,884
My hand is tired.

443
00:50:01,884 --> 00:50:03,073
The ink is thin.

444
00:50:03,073 --> 00:50:04,558
The light is fading.

445
00:50:04,558 --> 00:50:07,827
Thank goodness, it will soon be supper time.

446
00:50:09,127 --> 00:50:15,216
Tonight, the last scribe is finishing his work by the light of a single candle.

447
00:50:15,216 --> 00:50:18,376
He has copied his final line for the day.

448
00:50:18,376 --> 00:50:23,849
He wipes the quill, sets it down in its holder, and covers the inkwell.

449
00:50:23,849 --> 00:50:35,410
He looks over the page once more, the neat rows of letters, the space left for a painted capital that another brother will add later in gold and blue.

450
00:50:36,710 --> 00:50:43,387
Then he lays a cloth over the page to protect it, and stands, and stretches his stiff back.

451
00:50:43,387 --> 00:50:46,468
The book will wait for him until tomorrow.

452
00:50:46,468 --> 00:50:51,971
It has already waited for months, and it will wait patiently for many more.

453
00:50:51,971 --> 00:50:53,659
There is no hurry here.

454
00:50:53,659 --> 00:50:59,088
The work is slow, and steady, and it will be finished when it is finished.

455
00:51:00,389 --> 00:51:07,688
Along the walls of the room stand wooden chests and cupboards, where the finished books are kept.

456
00:51:07,688 --> 00:51:11,827
Each one represents months of quiet, careful attention.

457
00:51:11,827 --> 00:51:17,696
Lives of saints, books of psalms, works of history, of medicine, of astronomy.

458
00:51:17,696 --> 00:51:29,210
Many of the books that survive from the ancient world reached us because some patient scribe, in a room very much like this one, copied them out by hand.

459
00:51:38,510 --> 00:51:44,369
Beyond the cloister, through a little gate, lies the infirmary garden.

460
00:51:44,369 --> 00:51:52,404
Monasteries cared for their own sick and elderly, and often for travellers and local people too.

461
00:51:52,404 --> 00:51:58,179
And so they grew herbs, in neat raised beds, divided by narrow paths.

462
00:51:59,479 --> 00:52:04,722
In the dark, you cannot see the plants clearly, but you can smell them.

463
00:52:04,722 --> 00:52:09,891
Brush your hand along the edge of a bed, and the air fills with scent.

464
00:52:09,891 --> 00:52:10,261
Sage.

465
00:52:10,261 --> 00:52:10,925
Rosemary.

466
00:52:10,925 --> 00:52:11,295
Mint.

467
00:52:11,295 --> 00:52:11,959
Lavender.

468
00:52:11,959 --> 00:52:12,402
Thyme.

469
00:52:12,402 --> 00:52:17,719
Each herb had its uses, written down carefully in the monastery's books.

470
00:52:17,719 --> 00:52:27,319
Some for the stomach, some for coughs, some for sleep, and some simply because their fragrance made the sick room a gentler place.

471
00:52:28,619 --> 00:52:39,138
There is a famous old plan of an ideal monastery, drawn more than a thousand years ago, which still survives in a library in Switzerland.

472
00:52:39,138 --> 00:52:48,198
It shows every building in its place, and it even lists the herbs to be planted in the physician's garden, bed by bed.

473
00:52:48,198 --> 00:52:51,729
Lilies, roses, sage, rue, cumin, fennel, mint.

474
00:52:51,729 --> 00:52:58,870
Someone, long ago, sat down and imagined the whole peaceful place, right down to its flowers.

475
00:53:00,170 --> 00:53:07,773
At the edge of the garden stand a row of beehives, made of woven straw, shaped like round domes.

476
00:53:07,773 --> 00:53:10,465
Bees were precious to a monastery.

477
00:53:10,465 --> 00:53:13,633
Their honey sweetened food and medicine.

478
00:53:13,633 --> 00:53:23,850
And their wax made the best candles, which burned brightly and cleanly, with a soft, sweet smell, in the church and on the altar.

479
00:53:25,150 --> 00:53:28,823
At this hour, the bees are all inside, resting.

480
00:53:28,823 --> 00:53:35,934
If you lean close to one of the hives, you can hear a faint, low hum, like a sleeping town.

481
00:53:35,934 --> 00:53:46,249
Thousands of tiny lives, all gathered together in the warm dark, waiting for the morning sun to call them out again into the fields.

482
00:53:47,549 --> 00:53:52,922
The monk who looks after the bees has finished his work for the day.

483
00:53:52,922 --> 00:54:00,667
He walks slowly back along the garden path toward the church, his hands smelling of wax and herbs.

484
00:54:00,667 --> 00:54:03,512
Above him, the stars are very clear.

485
00:54:03,512 --> 00:54:09,202
A night bird calls from the orchard, once, twice, and then falls silent.

486
00:54:18,502 --> 00:54:21,145
Now the bell rings one more time.

487
00:54:21,145 --> 00:54:25,790
This is the call to Compline, the last service of the day.

488
00:54:25,790 --> 00:54:29,395
The word comes from the Latin for completion.

489
00:54:29,395 --> 00:54:36,763
It is the prayer that completes the day, and gently closes it, like the last page of a book.

490
00:54:38,063 --> 00:54:41,297
The monks gather in the church once more.

491
00:54:41,297 --> 00:54:47,213
Only a few candles are lit now, and most of the building is in deep shadow.

492
00:54:47,213 --> 00:54:56,837
Compline is short, and it is the same almost every night, so the monks know it by heart and barely need the light to read.

493
00:54:58,137 --> 00:55:03,077
The psalms they sing are about rest, and protection, and trust.

494
00:55:03,077 --> 00:55:13,587
Ancient words about lying down in peace and sleeping, about being sheltered through the night, about not being afraid of the darkness.

495
00:55:13,587 --> 00:55:15,704
The melody is soft and low.

496
00:55:15,704 --> 00:55:19,939
Some of the older monks close their eyes as they sing.

497
00:55:21,239 --> 00:55:29,120
Near the end, there is often a final song to the Virgin Mary, gentle and slow, sung in the near dark.

498
00:55:29,120 --> 00:55:41,293
Then the abbot gives a blessing, and the candles are put out, one by one, until only a single small light remains, burning near the altar through the night.

499
00:55:42,592 --> 00:55:46,741
And then begins what the monks call the Great Silence.

500
00:55:46,741 --> 00:55:51,735
From the end of Compline until the morning, no one speaks at all.

501
00:55:51,735 --> 00:55:52,580
Not a word.

502
00:55:52,580 --> 00:56:01,799
The rule of Benedict says that the monks should keep silence at all times, but especially during the hours of the night.

503
00:56:01,799 --> 00:56:06,102
Whatever was left unsaid today will wait until tomorrow.

504
00:56:07,402 --> 00:56:10,909
There is something very freeing in that.

505
00:56:10,909 --> 00:56:13,276
No conversations to finish.

506
00:56:13,276 --> 00:56:15,292
No questions to answer.

507
00:56:15,292 --> 00:56:17,133
No decisions to make.

508
00:56:17,133 --> 00:56:21,078
The day is over, and it has been handed back.

509
00:56:21,078 --> 00:56:25,813
Everyone simply turns, and walks quietly toward sleep.

510
00:56:27,112 --> 00:56:29,990
Let that silence settle over you too.

511
00:56:29,990 --> 00:56:35,358
Whatever was left unfinished in your own day can wait until tomorrow.

512
00:56:35,358 --> 00:56:39,402
For now, there is nothing to say, and nothing to do.

513
00:56:39,402 --> 00:56:46,014
There is only the quiet, and the dark, and the slow, steady rhythm of your breathing.

514
00:56:55,314 --> 00:57:04,102
The monks sleep together in one long room, the dormitory, up on the first floor of the building beside the church.

515
00:57:04,102 --> 00:57:14,663
The rule asked that they sleep in one place if possible, each in his own simple bed, with a light kept burning in the room until morning.

516
00:57:15,963 --> 00:57:17,445
The beds are plain.

517
00:57:17,445 --> 00:57:23,293
A wooden frame, a mattress stuffed with straw, a woollen blanket, a pillow.

518
00:57:23,293 --> 00:57:31,246
The monks sleep in their robes, so that they are ready to rise as soon as the bell rings in the night.

519
00:57:31,246 --> 00:57:38,342
The room is cool, and very quiet, and smells faintly of wool and wood smoke and candle wax.

520
00:57:39,642 --> 00:57:47,452
At one end of the dormitory there is a doorway that leads to a staircase, going straight down into the church.

521
00:57:47,452 --> 00:57:49,654
This is called the night stair.

522
00:57:49,654 --> 00:57:58,671
It meant that the monks could walk from their beds to the choir in the middle of the night without going outside into the cold.

523
00:57:58,671 --> 00:58:08,399
In a few old abbeys in England, these night stairs still survive, their stone steps worn into gentle hollows by centuries of sleepy feet.

524
00:58:09,699 --> 00:58:12,165
One by one, the monks lie down.

525
00:58:12,165 --> 00:58:15,665
A few whispered prayers, said without sound.

526
00:58:15,665 --> 00:58:18,927
A blanket pulled up against the cool air.

527
00:58:18,927 --> 00:58:24,336
Someone coughs softly at the far end of the room, and then is still.

528
00:58:24,336 --> 00:58:33,166
The single small lamp burns steadily on its ledge, and its light flickers very gently on the plain white walls.

529
00:58:34,466 --> 00:58:41,003
Through the narrow window, you can see a slice of the night sky, and a few bright stars.

530
00:58:41,003 --> 00:58:48,282
The moon has moved across the cloister by now, and its light is falling on the roof of the church.

531
00:58:48,282 --> 00:58:50,585
The whole monastery is resting.

532
00:58:50,585 --> 00:58:52,368
The bees in their hives.

533
00:58:52,368 --> 00:58:54,151
The horse in the stable.

534
00:58:54,151 --> 00:58:56,082
The books in their chests.

535
00:58:56,082 --> 00:58:57,865
The herbs in their beds.

536
00:58:57,865 --> 00:59:01,282
The stream, running on and on beyond the wall.

537
00:59:10,582 --> 00:59:11,490
Hours pass.

538
00:59:11,490 --> 00:59:14,049
The stars turn slowly overhead.

539
00:59:14,049 --> 00:59:20,901
And then, deep in the night, long before dawn, a small bell rings in the dormitory.

540
00:59:20,901 --> 00:59:28,331
It is time for the night office, the long prayer in the dark that the monks called Vigils.

541
00:59:29,631 --> 00:59:31,465
They rise without a word.

542
00:59:31,465 --> 00:59:39,460
They slip their feet into their shoes, and one after another, they walk down the night stair into the church.

543
00:59:39,460 --> 00:59:42,247
The candles are lit again, just a few.

544
00:59:42,247 --> 00:59:52,735
And in the depths of the night, while the whole countryside sleeps, the plainchant begins once more, slow and low, filling the dark stone room.

545
00:59:54,035 --> 00:59:58,616
For the monks, this was one of the most precious hours.

546
00:59:58,616 --> 01:00:01,781
The world outside was entirely asleep.

547
01:00:01,781 --> 01:00:03,947
No travellers on the road.

548
01:00:03,947 --> 01:00:04,697
No carts.

549
01:00:04,697 --> 01:00:05,530
No market.

550
01:00:05,530 --> 01:00:13,192
Only the quiet voices in the candlelight, keeping watch on behalf of everyone who could not.

551
01:00:14,493 --> 01:00:37,042
And when the office is over, the monks climb the night stair again, and return to their beds, and sleep a little longer, until the first grey light appears in the east, and the birds in the orchard begin to stir, and a new day starts, exactly like the last one, simple and slow and quiet.

552
01:00:38,342 --> 01:00:41,512
But that morning is still a long way off.

553
01:00:41,512 --> 01:00:44,528
For now, it is the middle of the night.

554
01:00:44,528 --> 01:00:47,930
The candles in the church have been put out.

555
01:00:47,930 --> 01:00:50,868
The cloister is silver with moonlight.

556
01:00:50,868 --> 01:00:58,523
And somewhere, in a quiet room, someone is keeping a small flame alive, so that you do not have to.

557
01:00:59,823 --> 01:01:01,389
You can let go now.

558
01:01:01,389 --> 01:01:04,852
Let the silence of the monastery hold you.

559
01:01:04,852 --> 01:01:08,397
Let the slow chant fade into the stillness.

560
01:01:08,397 --> 01:01:13,178
Let the night carry you gently, all the way until morning.

561
01:01:14,478 --> 01:01:19,303
Thank you for spending this evening with The Quiet Archive.

562
01:01:19,303 --> 01:01:20,121
Rest well.

563
01:01:20,121 --> 01:01:21,184
Sleep deeply.

564
01:01:21,184 --> 01:01:22,329
And goodnight.

565
01:02:18,666 --> 01:02:22,280
Hello again, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

566
01:02:22,280 --> 01:02:27,938
Tonight we are travelling a very long way, both in distance and in time.

567
01:02:27,938 --> 01:02:32,966
But there is no need to hurry, and nothing you need to remember.

568
01:02:32,966 --> 01:02:40,745
Just listen for as long as you like, and let the story drift on without you whenever you are ready.

569
01:02:42,045 --> 01:02:44,085
Make yourself comfortable.

570
01:02:44,085 --> 01:02:48,478
Feel the weight of your body settling down into the bed.

571
01:02:48,478 --> 01:02:50,517
Let your hands grow heavy.

572
01:02:50,517 --> 01:02:52,400
Let your shoulders drop.

573
01:02:52,400 --> 01:02:59,774
And breathe out slowly, the way warm air leaves a room when a window is opened in the evening.

574
01:03:01,074 --> 01:03:09,023
Tonight we are going to ancient Egypt, more than three thousand years ago, to the banks of the river Nile.

575
01:03:09,023 --> 01:03:16,148
We will spend a long, warm evening there, beside the water, among the palm trees and the reeds.

576
01:03:16,148 --> 01:03:27,847
We will visit a small village of craftsmen, share a supper on a flat roof under the stars, and listen to the old stories the Egyptians told about the night.

577
01:03:29,147 --> 01:03:31,401
It is the end of a hot day.

578
01:03:31,401 --> 01:03:35,157
The sun is sinking toward the western cliffs.

579
01:03:35,157 --> 01:03:39,664
And the air above the river is just beginning to cool.

580
01:03:48,964 --> 01:03:54,466
The Nile is wide here, and slow, and the colour of the evening sky.

581
01:03:54,466 --> 01:03:58,901
On both sides of it there is a narrow ribbon of green.

582
01:03:58,901 --> 01:04:06,292
Fields of wheat and barley, groves of date palms, gardens of onions and lettuce and beans.

583
01:04:06,292 --> 01:04:15,161
And beyond that green ribbon, on both sides, the desert begins, sudden and golden, rising toward low cliffs.

584
01:04:16,462 --> 01:04:19,582
The ancient Egyptians had names for this.

585
01:04:19,582 --> 01:04:25,596
The black land, for the dark, rich soil along the river, where everything grew.

586
01:04:25,596 --> 01:04:30,696
And the red land, for the desert beyond it, dry and empty and vast.

587
01:04:30,696 --> 01:04:39,373
Their whole world was this long, thin garden, running through the middle of the desert, fed entirely by the river.

588
01:04:40,674 --> 01:04:45,680
Along the bank, tall papyrus reeds grow thick in the shallow water.

589
01:04:45,680 --> 01:04:52,106
Their stems are smooth and green, with feathery heads that sway in the evening breeze.

590
01:04:52,106 --> 01:04:55,768
The Egyptians used papyrus for almost everything.

591
01:04:55,768 --> 01:05:08,770
They wove it into baskets and mats and sandals, tied it into bundles to make light boats, and pressed thin strips of it together to make the smooth pale sheets they wrote on.

592
01:05:10,069 --> 01:05:15,699
A small wooden boat glides past, its tall sail catching the last of the wind.

593
01:05:15,699 --> 01:05:22,498
On the Nile, the wind usually blows from the north, while the current flows toward the north.

594
01:05:22,498 --> 01:05:33,975
So a boat going south can raise its sail and let the wind carry it upstream, and a boat going north can simply lower the sail, and drift home on the current.

595
01:05:33,975 --> 01:05:41,067
The river and the wind together, working in opposite directions, like a slow and patient machine.

596
01:05:42,367 --> 01:05:46,583
On the far bank, a farmer is finishing his work at a shaduf.

597
01:05:46,583 --> 01:05:53,751
It is a long wooden pole balanced on a post, with a bucket at one end and a heavy weight at the other.

598
01:05:53,751 --> 01:06:03,801
He dips the bucket into the river, and the weight lifts it gently up, so he can swing it round and pour the water into a channel for his field.

599
01:06:03,801 --> 01:06:05,417
Dip, lift, swing, pour.

600
01:06:05,417 --> 01:06:07,033
Dip, lift, swing, pour.

601
01:06:07,033 --> 01:06:11,039
Then he sets it down, and wipes his face, and walks home.

602
01:06:12,339 --> 01:06:20,385
The sun touches the cliffs on the western horizon, and for a moment the whole river turns to gold.

603
01:06:20,385 --> 01:06:25,640
Then the light fades, quickly, the way it does in hot countries.

604
01:06:25,640 --> 01:06:31,634
The sky above the desert turns rose, then violet, then a deep, soft blue.

605
01:06:31,634 --> 01:06:34,590
And the first stars begin to appear.

606
01:06:43,889 --> 01:06:51,639
A little way from the river, in a quiet valley in the western hills, there is a small walled village.

607
01:06:51,639 --> 01:06:54,632
The people who live here are craftsmen.

608
01:06:54,632 --> 01:06:59,159
Stonecutters, plasterers, painters, sculptors, and scribes.

609
01:06:59,159 --> 01:07:07,676
Their work is to build and decorate the tombs of the kings, cut deep into the cliffs of a hidden valley nearby.

610
01:07:08,976 --> 01:07:16,916
Archaeologists call this village Deir el Medina, and it is one of the best known places in all of ancient Egypt.

611
01:07:16,916 --> 01:07:27,267
Because the people here could read and write unusually well, they left behind thousands of notes, scribbled on flakes of stone and broken pottery.

612
01:07:27,267 --> 01:07:28,330
Shopping lists.

613
01:07:28,330 --> 01:07:28,897
Letters.

614
01:07:28,897 --> 01:07:32,088
Records of who was absent from work, and why.

615
01:07:32,088 --> 01:07:35,490
One man stayed home because his wife was unwell.

616
01:07:35,490 --> 01:07:38,043
Another because he was brewing beer.

617
01:07:38,043 --> 01:07:41,445
Another because he had been stung by a scorpion.

618
01:07:42,745 --> 01:07:46,640
The houses stand close together along one main street.

619
01:07:46,640 --> 01:07:50,680
They are made of mud brick, plastered and painted white.

620
01:07:50,680 --> 01:07:54,719
Each has a few small rooms in a row, one behind another.

621
01:07:54,719 --> 01:07:58,830
A front room with a little shrine for the household gods.

622
01:07:58,830 --> 01:08:03,519
A main room with a raised bench and a column holding up the roof.

623
01:08:03,519 --> 01:08:09,289
A small kitchen at the back, open to the sky, with a clay oven for baking bread.

624
01:08:09,289 --> 01:08:12,463
And a staircase leading up to the flat roof.

625
01:08:13,763 --> 01:08:17,633
The workers did not go to the royal tombs every day.

626
01:08:17,633 --> 01:08:21,801
They worked in shifts, and their week was ten days long.

627
01:08:21,801 --> 01:08:33,634
They would spend their working days in the valley, sometimes sleeping in simple huts up on the ridge, and then come home to the village for their days of rest.

628
01:08:33,634 --> 01:08:36,760
Tonight, some of them have just come home.

629
01:08:38,060 --> 01:08:47,611
In the street, a few children are playing a game with small stones, under the watchful eye of a grandmother sitting in a doorway.

630
01:08:47,611 --> 01:08:51,165
A man carries a bundle of reeds on his shoulder.

631
01:08:51,165 --> 01:08:55,162
A cat slips along the top of a wall, silent and sleek.

632
01:08:55,162 --> 01:09:05,601
The Egyptians loved cats, and kept them in their homes, partly for company, and partly to keep away mice and snakes from the stores of grain.

633
01:09:06,901 --> 01:09:11,091
From inside the houses come the sounds of evening.

634
01:09:11,091 --> 01:09:18,129
The scrape of a grinding stone, as the last of the day's grain is turned into flour.

635
01:09:18,129 --> 01:09:19,889
The clink of pottery.

636
01:09:19,889 --> 01:09:20,810
Low voices.

637
01:09:20,810 --> 01:09:23,911
A baby fussing, and then going quiet.

638
01:09:23,911 --> 01:09:31,200
And drifting over everything, the warm, comforting smell of bread baking in clay ovens.

639
01:09:40,500 --> 01:09:46,575
Let us climb the narrow staircase of one of these houses, up onto the flat roof.

640
01:09:46,575 --> 01:09:51,739
In the hot months, the roof was the best place to be in the evening.

641
01:09:51,739 --> 01:09:56,371
It caught the breeze, and it was cooler than the rooms below.

642
01:09:56,371 --> 01:10:03,433
Families ate up here, and talked, and very often, they slept up here too, under the open sky.

643
01:10:04,733 --> 01:10:09,592
The roof is covered in mats woven from reeds and palm leaves.

644
01:10:09,592 --> 01:10:14,290
In one corner, some baskets of dates and onions are drying.

645
01:10:14,290 --> 01:10:21,538
In another, there is a low shelter made of palm fronds, to keep off the sun during the day.

646
01:10:21,538 --> 01:10:30,696
Now, in the evening, everyone sits out in the open, on the mats, with a few small oil lamps flickering beside them.

647
01:10:31,996 --> 01:10:34,007
Supper is simple, and good.

648
01:10:34,007 --> 01:10:36,836
Fresh bread, still warm from the oven.

649
01:10:36,836 --> 01:10:38,102
Onions and leeks.

650
01:10:38,102 --> 01:10:39,443
Beans and lentils.

651
01:10:39,443 --> 01:10:41,602
A handful of dates, and figs.

652
01:10:41,602 --> 01:10:45,549
Perhaps some fish from the river, or a little cheese.

653
01:10:45,549 --> 01:10:50,911
And to drink, beer, thick and mild and slightly sweet, made from barley.

654
01:10:50,911 --> 01:10:55,825
For the Egyptians, bread and beer were the most basic food of all.

655
01:10:55,825 --> 01:10:59,772
Workers were even paid partly in grain, to make them.

656
01:11:01,072 --> 01:11:07,720
The lamps are small dishes of clay, filled with oil, with a little twisted wick of linen.

657
01:11:07,720 --> 01:11:13,547
Their light is soft and golden, and it makes the faces around the circle glow.

658
01:11:13,547 --> 01:11:25,051
Beyond the edge of the roof, the village is dark, except for other small lights on other roofs, scattered like stars that have settled down for the night.

659
01:11:26,351 --> 01:11:28,138
Someone tells a story.

660
01:11:28,138 --> 01:11:29,763
Someone else laughs.

661
01:11:29,763 --> 01:11:39,431
A child, too tired to stay awake, curls up against her mother and falls asleep with a piece of bread still in her hand.

662
01:11:39,431 --> 01:11:45,850
Above them all, the sky is growing very dark, and very deep, and full of stars.

663
01:11:55,150 --> 01:11:59,210
The Egyptians watched the night sky with great care.

664
01:11:59,210 --> 01:12:04,832
And there was one star that mattered to them more than almost any other.

665
01:12:04,832 --> 01:12:06,238
We call it Sirius.

666
01:12:06,238 --> 01:12:09,986
It is the brightest star in the whole night sky.

667
01:12:09,986 --> 01:12:11,704
They called it Sopdet.

668
01:12:13,004 --> 01:12:19,637
For part of every year, Sirius cannot be seen at all, because it is too close to the sun.

669
01:12:19,637 --> 01:12:28,134
But then, one morning in early summer, it appears again, for the first time, low in the east, just before sunrise.

670
01:12:28,134 --> 01:12:34,991
The Egyptians noticed that this happened at about the same time that the Nile began to rise.

671
01:12:34,991 --> 01:12:38,866
And so the return of that bright star became a sign.

672
01:12:38,866 --> 01:12:40,357
The flood is coming.

673
01:12:40,357 --> 01:12:42,146
A new year is beginning.

674
01:12:43,445 --> 01:12:45,685
Every year, the Nile flooded.

675
01:12:45,685 --> 01:12:54,876
Rains far away to the south swelled the river, and slowly, over weeks, the water rose and spread out across the fields.

676
01:12:54,876 --> 01:13:00,668
When it went down again, it left behind a fresh layer of dark, fertile mud.

677
01:13:00,668 --> 01:13:04,375
That mud was the gift that made everything grow.

678
01:13:04,375 --> 01:13:09,472
Without the flood, there would have been no harvest, and no Egypt.

679
01:13:10,772 --> 01:13:18,136
The Egyptians divided their year into three seasons, each of four months, all shaped by the river.

680
01:13:18,136 --> 01:13:22,418
The season of the flood, when the fields lay under water.

681
01:13:22,418 --> 01:13:28,054
The season of growing, when the water went down and the seeds were planted.

682
01:13:28,054 --> 01:13:34,666
And the season of harvest, when the crops were gathered in, before the river rose again.

683
01:13:35,966 --> 01:13:40,609
During the flood season, farmers could not work their fields.

684
01:13:40,609 --> 01:13:48,069
So many of them went to work on great building projects instead, or rested, or mended their tools.

685
01:13:48,069 --> 01:14:01,011
Imagine the whole valley turned into a shining lake, with villages standing on little islands of higher ground, and boats moving slowly between them in the evening light.

686
01:14:02,311 --> 01:14:04,918
Tonight, the river is low and calm.

687
01:14:04,918 --> 01:14:09,612
It is the season of harvest, and the fields are golden and dry.

688
01:14:09,612 --> 01:14:13,337
The great flood will not come for some months yet.

689
01:14:13,337 --> 01:14:19,074
But up in the sky, the stars turn slowly, keeping their ancient appointments.

690
01:14:19,074 --> 01:14:31,367
And one day, before dawn, Sopdet will rise again in the east, and the river will begin to swell, just as it has done every year, for longer than anyone can remember.

691
01:14:40,667 --> 01:14:45,644
Across the river, on the eastern bank, stands a great temple.

692
01:14:45,644 --> 01:14:52,823
Its walls are enormous, built of sandstone blocks, carved with scenes of gods and kings.

693
01:14:52,823 --> 01:15:04,816
In front of it rises a huge gateway with two sloping towers, and before the gateway, tall flagpoles and stone obelisks point up into the night sky.

694
01:15:06,116 --> 01:15:11,473
During the day, ordinary people could not go very far into the temple.

695
01:15:11,473 --> 01:15:15,147
The deepest rooms were reserved for the priests.

696
01:15:15,147 --> 01:15:17,749
But at night, it is quieter still.

697
01:15:17,749 --> 01:15:20,428
Most of the priests have gone home.

698
01:15:20,428 --> 01:15:28,004
A few remain, to watch over the sanctuary, and to perform the rituals that kept the world in order.

699
01:15:29,304 --> 01:15:39,428
Inside, the great hall is filled with columns, taller than trees, so thick that several people holding hands could not reach around them.

700
01:15:39,428 --> 01:15:49,551
They are carved and painted to look like bundles of papyrus, so that walking through the hall is like walking through a vast stone marsh.

701
01:15:49,551 --> 01:15:56,867
In the lamplight, the painted reeds and flowers on the columns glow faintly, red and blue and gold.

702
01:15:58,167 --> 01:16:07,385
The priests here washed themselves in a sacred lake beside the temple, several times a day, to be pure before the gods.

703
01:16:07,385 --> 01:16:11,491
Tonight, the surface of that lake is perfectly still.

704
01:16:11,491 --> 01:16:20,012
The stars are reflected in it, and the dark shape of the temple walls, and a single lamp burning in a doorway.

705
01:16:21,312 --> 01:16:28,151
Somewhere deep inside, a priest moves quietly through the rooms, carrying a small lamp.

706
01:16:28,151 --> 01:16:30,981
He checks that the doors are closed.

707
01:16:30,981 --> 01:16:33,653
He adjusts an offering of flowers.

708
01:16:33,653 --> 01:16:42,536
He pauses before a carving of the goddess of the sky, her body arched across the whole ceiling, covered in stars.

709
01:16:42,536 --> 01:16:47,488
Then he walks on, his bare feet silent on the cool stone floor.

710
01:16:56,788 --> 01:17:02,397
Far to the north, many days downriver by boat, stand the great pyramids.

711
01:17:02,397 --> 01:17:07,695
By the time our story takes place, they were already incredibly old.

712
01:17:07,695 --> 01:17:15,017
The largest of them, built for a king named Khufu, was already well over a thousand years old.

713
01:17:15,017 --> 01:17:23,353
To the people of this village, the pyramids were as ancient as the oldest castles and cathedrals are to us.

714
01:17:23,353 --> 01:17:24,522
Older, in fact.

715
01:17:25,822 --> 01:17:33,954
Travellers who sailed down the Nile would see them rising on the western horizon, at the edge of the desert.

716
01:17:33,954 --> 01:17:37,869
Huge, smooth, pale shapes, standing against the sky.

717
01:17:37,869 --> 01:17:51,272
In those days, they were still covered in a casing of fine white limestone, polished and smooth, so that they would have shone in the sunlight, and glowed faintly under the moon.

718
01:17:52,572 --> 01:17:57,735
Even the Egyptians themselves visited them as ancient monuments.

719
01:17:57,735 --> 01:18:08,382
Some left notes scratched on the walls of old temples nearby, saying that they had come to admire the work of the kings of long ago.

720
01:18:08,382 --> 01:18:14,674
Tourists, three thousand years ago, marvelling at the past, just as we do now.

721
01:18:15,974 --> 01:18:19,633
Picture them tonight, far away in the darkness.

722
01:18:19,633 --> 01:18:21,658
Silent, enormous, patient.

723
01:18:21,658 --> 01:18:24,772
The moon moving slowly over their faces.

724
01:18:24,772 --> 01:18:28,744
The desert wind brushing softly across their sides.

725
01:18:28,744 --> 01:18:37,776
They have stood there through thousands of nights exactly like this one, and they will stand through thousands more.

726
01:18:47,076 --> 01:18:51,211
The Egyptians told a beautiful story about the night.

727
01:18:51,211 --> 01:19:00,340
They believed that the sun was carried across the sky each day in a boat, sailing over the heavens from east to west.

728
01:19:00,340 --> 01:19:06,894
And each evening, when the sun set behind the western cliffs, the boat did not stop.

729
01:19:06,894 --> 01:19:09,156
It sailed on, into the night.

730
01:19:10,456 --> 01:19:19,882
The story goes that during the hours of darkness, the sun's boat travelled through a hidden world beneath the earth, called the Duat.

731
01:19:19,882 --> 01:19:31,575
The night was divided into twelve hours, and each hour was like a different region of that hidden world, with its own gates and guardians, its own rivers and fields.

732
01:19:31,575 --> 01:19:37,315
The boat passed through them one by one, slowly, steadily, all through the night.

733
01:19:38,615 --> 01:19:49,666
And at the end of the twelfth hour, the boat reached the eastern horizon, and the sun rose again, renewed and shining, to begin a new day.

734
01:19:49,666 --> 01:19:53,670
Every sunrise, in this story, was a small rebirth.

735
01:19:53,670 --> 01:19:59,436
The night was not an ending, but a journey, and the morning always came.

736
01:20:00,736 --> 01:20:05,342
The Egyptians also used the stars to tell the time at night.

737
01:20:05,342 --> 01:20:12,559
They watched for particular groups of stars rising, one after another, through the dark hours.

738
01:20:12,559 --> 01:20:24,075
Priests whose job was to keep the temple rituals on time would note which stars had appeared, and so they would know how much of the night had passed.

739
01:20:25,375 --> 01:20:30,217
There is something very comforting in their idea of the night.

740
01:20:30,217 --> 01:20:34,747
That while we sleep, the sun is not gone, only travelling.

741
01:20:34,747 --> 01:20:41,073
That the darkness is a passage, carefully guided, hour by hour, toward the light.

742
01:20:41,073 --> 01:20:47,711
That somewhere beneath the quiet earth, a little boat is sailing on, and all is well.

743
01:20:57,011 --> 01:21:01,791
Back on the roof in the village, the lamps are burning low.

744
01:21:01,791 --> 01:21:03,654
The stories have ended.

745
01:21:03,654 --> 01:21:09,730
The children are all asleep, rolled in light linen sheets on the reed mats.

746
01:21:09,730 --> 01:21:17,832
The grown ups are lying down too, one by one, as the cool night air finally settles over the valley.

747
01:21:19,132 --> 01:21:23,280
Down in the street below, the last door is closed.

748
01:21:23,280 --> 01:21:27,097
A dog barks once, far away, and then is quiet.

749
01:21:27,097 --> 01:21:37,052
The cat finds a warm spot on a wall that is still holding the heat of the day, curls into a circle, and closes its eyes.

750
01:21:38,352 --> 01:21:44,821
Beyond the village, the river flows on in the darkness, wide and slow and silent.

751
01:21:44,821 --> 01:21:49,534
The papyrus reeds whisper as the breeze moves through them.

752
01:21:49,534 --> 01:21:52,888
A heron stands motionless in the shallows.

753
01:21:52,888 --> 01:22:00,795
The little boats are pulled up on the bank, their sails folded, rocking very slightly on the water.

754
01:22:02,095 --> 01:22:04,766
Above it all, the sky is immense.

755
01:22:04,766 --> 01:22:10,919
Thousands upon thousands of stars, from one edge of the desert to the other.

756
01:22:10,919 --> 01:22:17,800
The Milky Way stretches across it like a river of light, a mirror of the river below.

757
01:22:17,800 --> 01:22:26,543
The Egyptians saw the sky goddess in that great arch of stars, bending protectively over the sleeping earth.

758
01:22:27,843 --> 01:22:34,802
Somewhere beneath the horizon, the boat of the sun is sailing through the hours of the night.

759
01:22:34,802 --> 01:22:38,019
It will arrive in the east in its own time.

760
01:22:38,019 --> 01:22:41,236
There is nothing you need to do to help it.

761
01:22:41,236 --> 01:22:48,046
You only need to rest, and let the night pass gently, and trust that the morning will come.

762
01:22:49,346 --> 01:22:53,189
Let the warm air of the Nile settle around you.

763
01:22:53,189 --> 01:22:56,215
Let the sound of the river carry you.

764
01:22:56,215 --> 01:23:05,047
Let the stars watch over you, as they watched over everyone who ever slept on those flat roofs, so long ago.

765
01:23:06,347 --> 01:23:11,172
Thank you for spending this evening with The Quiet Archive.

766
01:23:11,172 --> 01:23:11,990
Rest well.

767
01:23:11,990 --> 01:23:13,053
Sleep deeply.

768
01:23:13,053 --> 01:23:14,198
And goodnight.
